## 🎤 Top Interview Questions

### Q1: TypeScript kya hai?
> "TypeScript JavaScript ka **superset** hai jo **static type checking** add karta hai.
> Ye compile hoke JavaScript me convert hota hai.
> Iska main fayda hai ki bugs ko **development time** pe hi pakad leta hai,
> production me crash hone se bacha leta hai."

### Q2: Interface aur Type me kya farq hai?
```typescript
// Interface — sirf objects ke liye, extendable
interface User { name: string; }
interface Admin extends User { level: number; }

// Type — kuch bhi ban sakta hai
type ID = string | number;               // Union
type Point = { x: number; y: number };   // Object
type Status = "active" | "inactive";     // Literal
```

### Q3: `any` vs `unknown` vs `never` kya hai?
```typescript
let a: any = 5;      a = "hi"; a.xyz();  // ✅ Sab allowed (UNSAFE)
let b: unknown = 5;  b.xyz();            // ❌ Pehle check karo type
let c: never;        c = 5;              // ❌ Koi value assign nahi ho sakti
```
> - `any` = TS ka type system OFF kar do (avoid karo)
> - `unknown` = Safe version of any (pehle check karo fir use karo)
> - `never` = Ye kabhi nahi hoga (empty state, infinite loop, throw)

### Q4: Generics kya hain?
```typescript
// Bina Generic (Restricted):
function getFirst(arr: number[]): number { return arr[0]; }
// Sirf numbers ke liye kaam karega!

// Generic ke saath (Flexible):
function getFirst<T>(arr: T[]): T { return arr[0]; }
getFirst([1, 2, 3]);        // T = number, return: number
getFirst(["a", "b"]);       // T = string, return: string
// Ek function, har type ke liye kaam karta hai!
```

> Tumhare project me: `Schema<IUser>`, `mongoose.model<IUser>()` — ye Generic hai!

### Q5: Type Assertion (`as`) kya hai?
```typescript
// Jab TUMHE pata hai ki type kya hai, par TS ko nahi pata
process.env.JWT_SECRET           // Type: string | undefined
process.env.JWT_SECRET as string // Type: string (Tum guarantee de rahe ho)

// ⚠️ Risk: Agar galat guarantee di toh RUNTIME crash hoga!
```

### Q6: Optional Chaining (`?.`) kya hai?
```typescript
// Pehle (Bina ?.):
if (req.user && req.user._id) { ... }  // Lambi check

// Ab (?.):
if (req.user?._id) { ... }  // Short & safe!
// Agar req.user undefined hai toh ._id check hi nahi karega
// undefined return kar dega bina crash kiye
```

### Q7: JWT (JSON Web Token) kaise kaam karta hai?
```
LOGIN:
User credentials → Server verify → JWT Token generate → Client ko do

PROTECTED ROUTES:
Client request + Token → Server verify token → Data decode → Access allow

Token Structure:
  header.payload.signature
  eyJhbG.eyJ1c2Vy.SflKxw

  Header: Algorithm info (HS256)
  Payload: User data ({user: {...}})
  Signature: Secret se sign kiya (tamper-proof)
```

### Q8: Middleware kya hai Express me?
```
Request → Middleware 1 → Middleware 2 → Controller → Response
              ↓              ↓
        (express.json)    (isAuth)
        Body parse karo   Token check karo

Middleware = Beech me baithne wala function jo request ko 
             process/filter karta hai controller tak pahuchne se pehle.
```

### Q9: Higher-Order Function kya hai? (Tumhare TryCatch jaise)
```typescript
// Ye function ek FUNCTION ko input me leta hai
// Aur ek NAYA FUNCTION return karta hai
const TryCatch = (handler) => {      // Input: function
    return async (req, res, next) => { // Output: new function
        try { await handler(req, res, next); }
        catch(err) { res.status(500).json({...}); }
    }
}

// Real examples: Array.map(), Array.filter(), setTimeout()
[1,2,3].map((x) => x * 2);  // map() ek function le raha hai
```

### Q10: `var` vs `let` vs `const` kya hai?
```javascript
var x = 1;   // Function-scoped, hoisted, re-declare allowed (OLD, avoid it)
let y = 2;   // Block-scoped, NOT hoisted, re-assign allowed
const z = 3; // Block-scoped, NOT hoisted, NOT re-assignable (BEST for constants)
```
